* ephem database.** these are sorted for easy reading but they don't have to be in any order.* things will run faster if you put the objects you use nearer the top.** elliptical format: inclination, longitude of ascending node,*   argument of perihelion, mean distance (aka semi-major axis),*   daily motion, eccentricity, mean anomaly (ie, degrees from perihelion),*   epoch date (ie, time of M), the equinox year (ie, time of i/O/o).*   absolute magnitude, magnitude slope parameter.** parabolic format: epoch of perihelion, inclination, argument of perihelion,*   perihelion distance, longitude of the ascending node, reference epoch of*   the parameters, absolute magnitude, luminosity index** fixed format: ra, dec, magnitude, reference epoch* from IAU circular 4985Austin,p,4/9.9715/1990,58.9574,61.5625,0.349854,75.2223,1950.0,4.5,4* from IAU circular 4986Cernis,p,3/17.2967/1990,48.138,100.588,1.06849,347.727,1950,0,0* from IAU circular 4984George,p,4/11.9396/1990,59.3652,137.8482,1.569001,279.3118,1950,0,0Vesta,e,7.139,104.015,149.986,2.3607,0.27174,0.0906,152.190,11/5/1990,2000.0,3.16,0.34Ceres,e,10.607,80.702,71.274,2.7685,0.21396,0.0780,287.265,10/1/1989,2000.0,3.32,0.11Halley,e,162.238,58.154,111.857,17.9435,0.0129674,0.9673,0,1986.109,1950,3.66,7.05Pallas,e,34.804,173.323,309.796,2.7703,0.21375,0.2347,273.779,10/1/1989,2000.0,4.13,0.15Eunomia,e,11.76001,292.89583,97.40910,2.6446692,0.22916434,0.1850083,236.28800,8/27/1988,1950.0,5.22,0.20Eunomia,e,11.765,293.619,97.591,2.6437,0.22929,0.1849,327.856,10/1/1989,2000.0,5.22,0.20Juno,e,12.991,170.542,246.787,2.6692,0.22601,0.2575,205.808,11/5/1990,2000.0,5.31,0.30Hygiea,e,3.840,283.833,315.832,3.1365,0.17743,0.1202,104.089,11/5/1990,2000.0,5.37,0.15Iris,e,5.513,260.100,144.725,2.3864,0.26735,0.2292,239.261,11/5/1990,2000.0,5.56,0.25Hebe,e,14.781,139.033,238.480,2.4250,0.26100,0.2020,253.786,11/5/1990,2000.0,5.70,0.24Herculina,e,16.35677,107.39552,75.08723,2.7711409,0.21365652,0.1763770,